What do I mean by that?

00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:38.240
So the olden days,

00:01:38.241 --> 00:01:42.040
data science meant that
you collect data once and

00:01:42.041 --> 00:01:45.435
you analyze it and then you
go home and the world ends.

00:01:45.436 --> 00:01:50.340
So this look like things
like the set senses,

00:01:50.341 --> 00:01:54.280
I get new data every 10 years.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.690
Maybe like a survey that
was mailed out and you

00:01:57.691 --> 00:01:59.984
had to first build a survey,

00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:01.395
mail it out and then wait

00:02:01.396 --> 00:02:03.645
three months to get
the responses back,

00:02:03.646 --> 00:02:05.400
or if you're a very fancy,

00:02:05.401 --> 00:02:08.820
maybe there was a call center
and you had to like train

00:02:08.821 --> 00:02:11.190
these people to call and

00:02:11.191 --> 00:02:14.150
ask very specific questions
and they collected data,

00:02:14.151 --> 00:02:16.255
and you analyze it at the end.

00:02:16.256 --> 00:02:19.290
Modern data science
looks very different.

00:02:19.291 --> 00:02:23.890
It happens online and it's
changing all the time.

00:02:23.891 --> 00:02:26.260
We're now doing things like

00:02:26.261 --> 00:02:28.150
collecting everyone's health
information and

00:02:28.151 --> 00:02:32.775
their tweets and
their social behavior online,

00:02:32.776 --> 00:02:34.650
and maps, and so we now

00:02:34.651 --> 00:02:36.325
have to do like

00:02:36.326 --> 00:02:38.350
a continuous analysis
of a growing database,

00:02:38.351 --> 00:02:41.485
because we're acquiring
more data over time.

00:02:41.486 --> 00:02:43.975
We also can'T wait until we

00:02:43.976 --> 00:02:46.720
accumulate all the tweets
in the world and

00:02:46.721 --> 00:02:48.940
then decide what's trending,

00:02:48.941 --> 00:02:50.200
but in fact I have to tell

00:02:50.201 --> 00:02:52.865
you all the time on
the current database

00:02:52.866 --> 00:02:57.165
the current tweets and
the current trends.

00:02:57.166 --> 00:03:00.000
So, we need new analysis tools

00:03:00.001 --> 00:03:02.055
for the case of
growing databases.

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:03.525
But in these examples,

00:03:03.526 --> 00:03:05.850
it's also like
highly sensitive data,

00:03:05.851 --> 00:03:08.265
and so this is
your health information,

00:03:08.266 --> 00:03:11.295
and this is your social data,

00:03:11.296 --> 00:03:13.005
and your GPS data,

00:03:13.006 --> 00:03:15.960
and so we also need some kind
of privacy guarantees,

00:03:15.961 --> 00:03:18.180
because we're now collecting
much more sensitive,

00:03:18.181 --> 00:03:21.070
much more personal
information about people.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.590
So, some background,
differential privacy was

00:03:25.591 --> 00:03:27.450
defined in 2006 byte work

00:03:27.451 --> 00:03:29.765
makes sharing nice Siemens Smith,

00:03:29.766 --> 00:03:31.960
and it informally bounds

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:34.480
the maximum amount the
one person's data can

00:03:34.481 --> 00:03:36.160
change the output of

00:03:36.161 --> 00:03:39.805
some computation performed
on a very large database.

00:03:39.806 --> 00:03:42.130
Formally it says this,

00:03:42.131 --> 00:03:44.630
"So, an algorithm M,

00:03:44.631 --> 00:03:48.300
the maps from an N tuple
of types," and so think of

00:03:48.301 --> 00:03:51.675
that as a data collected
from N different people,

00:03:51.676 --> 00:03:53.640
just an arbitrary range is

00:03:53.641 --> 00:03:56.505
parametrized Epsilon
differential private.

00:03:56.506 --> 00:03:59.640
If it's the case that for
all neighboring databases,

00:03:59.641 --> 00:04:02.955
they're the same except for
one person's data and then an

00:04:02.956 --> 00:04:05.055
output approximately
the same thing

00:04:05.056 --> 00:04:07.200
on these two
neighboring databases.

00:04:07.201 --> 00:04:09.900
By approximately, I mean
we're going to compare

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:13.245
the probability that
the output anything S,

00:04:13.246 --> 00:04:17.850
and the probability
that I produce S should

00:04:17.851 --> 00:04:20.520
be multiplicatively close up

00:04:20.521 --> 00:04:23.785
to this e to the Epsilon factor.

00:04:23.786 --> 00:04:26.135
So, in pictures it
looks like this,

00:04:26.136 --> 00:04:28.345
I have some blue database

00:04:28.346 --> 00:04:31.430
and they produce
some blue curve over there,

00:04:31.431 --> 00:04:34.320
and I'm then going
to change person i's

00:04:34.321 --> 00:04:37.530
data from t_i to being t_i prime,

00:04:37.531 --> 00:04:39.840
and then going to get
the red curve instead.

00:04:39.841 --> 00:04:42.435
So, these things are close.

00:04:42.436 --> 00:04:47.065
So, I can then bound
the impact of person i's data.

00:04:47.066 --> 00:04:48.925
So really I'm saying,

00:04:48.926 --> 00:04:52.260
if you provide your data
or if you don'T,

00:04:52.261 --> 00:04:54.315
if you're truthful or if you lie,

00:04:54.316 --> 00:04:56.515
I'm going to learn
about the same thing

00:04:56.516 --> 00:04:58.840
anyways until your portion of

00:04:58.841 --> 00:05:02.160
my dataset has
a very small effect

00:05:02.161 --> 00:05:06.375
on the thing that I learn
at the end of the day.

00:05:06.376 --> 00:05:09.105
So, think of this set S as

00:05:09.106 --> 00:05:11.535
the collection of
all bad outcomes,

00:05:11.536 --> 00:05:13.350
maybe this is like I'm going to

00:05:13.351 --> 00:05:15.570
analyze our health data to

00:05:15.571 --> 00:05:17.550
decide insurance rates
and decide if

00:05:17.551 --> 00:05:19.840
like smoking causes lung cancer.

00:05:19.841 --> 00:05:23.460
Like saying like
your insurance price

00:05:23.461 --> 00:05:25.230
is going to rise to
the same probability

00:05:25.231 --> 00:05:27.450
if you share your data
versus if you don't T.

00:05:27.451 --> 00:05:30.330
So think of this as
like the collection of

00:05:30.331 --> 00:05:32.130
all like bad outcomes that may

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:34.725
happen as a result of
the analysis of this data,

00:05:34.726 --> 00:05:37.530
and then saying, those things
are going to happen

00:05:37.531 --> 00:05:40.170
with about the same
probability anyways,

00:05:40.171 --> 00:05:42.915
and so I'm really not
relying on your data.

00:05:42.916 --> 00:05:47.070
It's also a very strong like
worst-case guarantee.

00:05:47.071 --> 00:05:48.360
So it's worst-case over

00:05:48.361 --> 00:05:50.325
all pairs of
neighboring databases,

00:05:50.326 --> 00:05:54.345
and over all collections of
all possible bad outcomes,

00:05:54.346 --> 00:05:56.740
and so this is very very strong.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:03.855
Any questions so far?
Okay. There's a couple of

00:06:03.856 --> 00:06:05.730
nice properties that make

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:08.580
differential privacy desirable as

00:06:08.581 --> 00:06:11.140
a tool for designing algorithms.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.225
So first, it's robust
to post-processing.

00:06:15.226 --> 00:06:18.960
So if I have some differential
private algorithm M,

00:06:18.961 --> 00:06:23.980
and I then apply
any other function to it,

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.330
the post process thing
is still going to have

00:06:27.331 --> 00:06:29.715
the same differential
privacy guarantee,

00:06:29.716 --> 00:06:32.940
and so really this
says no adversary can

00:06:32.941 --> 00:06:37.170
take the output of my
differential private algorithm,

00:06:37.171 --> 00:06:39.345
going to corner,
think really hard,

00:06:39.346 --> 00:06:41.640
run some additional
computations on

00:06:41.641 --> 00:06:44.620
it and then like reverse
engineer my data.

00:06:44.621 --> 00:06:46.800
So, I'm promising no adversary

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:49.390
can break this privacy guarantee.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.880
So, note also, there's
no requirements

00:06:53.881 --> 00:06:56.805
on the computational
power of the adversary,

00:06:56.806 --> 00:06:57.720
the rather this is

00:06:57.721 --> 00:07:03.400
a like information
theoretic guarantee.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:07.215
It also well composes adaptively.

00:07:07.216 --> 00:07:10.680
So, this says if I have K

00:07:10.681 --> 00:07:16.170
different private mechanisms and

00:07:16.171 --> 00:07:20.270
they're each Epsilon i
differentially private,

00:07:20.271 --> 00:07:22.520
then if I were to
compose all K of these,

00:07:22.521 --> 00:07:25.280
then I can just add up
my Epsilons and that gives

00:07:25.281 --> 00:07:29.050
me my final privacy guarantee.

00:07:29.051 --> 00:07:30.590
So, really if I want to run

00:07:30.591 --> 00:07:32.120
a whole bunch of
different Epsilon

00:07:32.121 --> 00:07:35.375
differentially private
computations on the same data,

00:07:35.376 --> 00:07:38.410
the Epsilons are just
going to add up.

00:07:38.411 --> 00:07:41.690
There's more advanced
versions of this that

00:07:41.691 --> 00:07:44.180
I won'T include
today, for example,

00:07:44.181 --> 00:07:47.995
this still holds if you choose
these M_i's adaptively,

00:07:47.996 --> 00:07:51.840
how you first run M_1
and see the output of

00:07:51.841 --> 00:07:53.160
that and then decide on which

00:07:53.161 --> 00:07:56.950
M_2 to run next, and
this still holds.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.865
Here, there's also like I'm,

00:08:02.866 --> 00:08:06.390
relaxation of this where you have

00:08:06.391 --> 00:08:11.010
an additional delta
term over here,

00:08:11.011 --> 00:08:12.480
as like an additive slack,

00:08:12.481 --> 00:08:16.560
and so I then have this
multiplicative parameter Epsilon,

00:08:16.561 --> 00:08:19.695
and that I have as
an additive slack delta.

00:08:19.696 --> 00:08:23.220
I won'T talk about it
today but there'll be

00:08:23.221 --> 00:08:25.840
some like brief mentions in

00:08:25.841 --> 00:08:28.795
passing about this like
Epsilon delta version,

00:08:28.796 --> 00:08:31.600
and so just keep in mind
that's what the Epsilon Delta

00:08:31.601 --> 00:08:35.820
is there's this like
additive delta term here.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:41.290
So, there is a more advanced
composition version where

00:08:41.291 --> 00:08:44.010
the Epsilons don'T
just add up but in

00:08:44.011 --> 00:08:47.445
fact it looks like
the L_2 norm of the Epsilons,

00:08:47.446 --> 00:08:53.910
and so you get
much better composition

00:08:53.911 --> 00:08:55.570
in the Epsilons where you have to

00:08:55.571 --> 00:08:57.910
have is like additive loss
in the delta.

00:08:57.911 --> 00:09:01.810
So, you can do better than this,

00:09:01.811 --> 00:09:06.370
but this is sort of like
the most simple version.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:13.610
Okay. So, back to why
differential privacy is great.

00:09:13.611 --> 00:09:17.020
So, because we have this
parameterized privacy notion

00:09:17.021 --> 00:09:19.685
I can now move smoothly between
informational extremes.

00:09:19.686 --> 00:09:21.080
I no longer have to

00:09:21.081 --> 00:09:23.910
say that it's either
going to give you data or

00:09:23.911 --> 00:09:25.730
not but I can actually like

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:27.870
quantify how much
information am I

00:09:27.871 --> 00:09:30.705
leaking to my epsilon

00:09:30.706 --> 00:09:34.930
by performing this particular
computation on my dataset.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.400
We saw that it has these like a

00:09:40.401 --> 00:09:43.749
post-processing in these
composition guarantees.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:45.470
Which mean if you want to

00:09:45.471 --> 00:09:48.790
design differential private
algorithms then you only

00:09:48.791 --> 00:09:50.200
have to reason about

00:09:50.201 --> 00:09:54.040
these like very simple
subroutines that are

00:09:54.041 --> 00:09:56.920
private and then you can
just add up the epsilons

00:09:56.921 --> 00:09:59.870
in your entire larger more
complicated algorithm.

00:09:59.871 --> 00:10:05.390
It's being used in
practice and in theory,

00:10:05.391 --> 00:10:07.495
it's being used here.

00:10:07.496 --> 00:10:09.565
Thanks guys for inviting me.

00:10:09.566 --> 00:10:10.970
So it's being used in practice

00:10:10.971 --> 00:10:12.875
by Apple, Google, Microsoft,

00:10:12.876 --> 00:10:16.945
Uber and these census bureau.

00:10:16.946 --> 00:10:20.225
We now have a rich background of

00:10:20.226 --> 00:10:23.040
a differentiate private
algorithms that are both private

00:10:23.041 --> 00:10:26.610
and have some usefulness
guaranteed. So it seems great.

00:10:26.611 --> 00:10:29.380
The problem is nearly everything

00:10:29.381 --> 00:10:32.040
that's been done operates
on a static database.

00:10:32.041 --> 00:10:37.870
So we have great tools
for static database,

00:10:37.871 --> 00:10:42.290
we have virtually nothing
for growing database.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.665
Previous work only
allowed us to count

00:10:45.666 --> 00:10:49.310
bits in a stream and that's
all we could do privately.

00:10:49.311 --> 00:10:51.460
So today we're going to do

00:10:51.461 --> 00:10:54.270
a lot more stuff privately
on a growing database.

00:10:54.271 --> 00:10:56.960
First we're going to

00:10:56.961 --> 00:10:59.290
see a very specific tool
for answering

00:10:59.291 --> 00:11:04.640
adaptive linear queries
and that's going to

00:11:04.641 --> 00:11:10.990
be based on the algorithm
private duplicative weights.

00:11:10.991 --> 00:11:12.920
And we'll see why it can'T just

00:11:12.921 --> 00:11:15.940
extend naturally to
the growing database case.

00:11:15.941 --> 00:11:17.570
We'll show how to improve it

00:11:17.571 --> 00:11:19.780
to make it work and
then we'll talk about

00:11:19.781 --> 00:11:24.640
much more a general purpose
results and how we can

00:11:24.641 --> 00:11:26.855
extend existing
accuracy and privacy

00:11:26.856 --> 00:11:28.565
guarantees in the static case

00:11:28.566 --> 00:11:30.580
into the growing environment.

00:11:30.581 --> 00:11:34.970
Finally, this is a first paper

00:11:34.971 --> 00:11:36.705
on this topic and I think
that there's lots of

00:11:36.706 --> 00:11:38.810
other types of growth

00:11:38.811 --> 00:11:40.580
to be analyzed and
so I'll talk more at

00:11:40.581 --> 00:11:45.450
the end about questions
raised by this work.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.780
Any more question
where I jump in?

00:11:49.781 --> 00:11:53.350
Cool. Okay so the first part is

00:11:53.351 --> 00:11:57.070
on private multiplicative
weights for growing databases.

00:11:57.071 --> 00:12:01.619
This is a great algorithm
to answer exponentially

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.500
many adaptively chosen
queries and so

00:12:03.501 --> 00:12:06.545
that's a super powerful tool

00:12:06.546 --> 00:12:11.170
but it doesn'T naturally
extend to growing databases.

00:12:11.171 --> 00:12:15.695
Just one slide of that
preliminaries first.

00:12:15.696 --> 00:12:19.030
So, for this part we're going
to store our database as

00:12:19.031 --> 00:12:22.889
a histogram and so think
of a database as a vector,

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:26.010
where the Ith entry
is the fraction of

00:12:26.011 --> 00:12:29.990
the entries in the database
that are of data type I.

00:12:29.991 --> 00:12:33.740
So there's N total types of
data and I'm just going to

00:12:33.741 --> 00:12:37.625
count how many of each
type have I seen.

00:12:37.626 --> 00:12:40.640
So for example if this
is a binary data,

00:12:40.641 --> 00:12:43.540
I want to count like do
you have a disease or not.

00:12:43.541 --> 00:12:45.910
There's two types and you
can either have a plus or

00:12:45.911 --> 00:12:47.610
minus and that's it and so

00:12:47.611 --> 00:12:50.300
then this is going to be a tuple.

00:12:50.301 --> 00:12:54.540
Maybe I want to count
how many people of which

00:12:54.541 --> 00:12:57.150
each age they are
and so then this is

00:12:57.151 --> 00:13:00.965
maybe 120 if I'm being
generous. Things like that.

00:13:00.966 --> 00:13:04.000
So, we're going to be
thinking in this case about

00:13:04.001 --> 00:13:06.990
just answering linear queries

00:13:06.991 --> 00:13:09.195
where each query it's going to

00:13:09.196 --> 00:13:11.760
also be described as
a vector and it will

00:13:11.761 --> 00:13:16.865
assign a weight to
each type of data X_I.

00:13:16.866 --> 00:13:19.400
We're going to assume that
these weights are bounded

00:13:19.401 --> 00:13:22.630
between zero and one.

00:13:22.631 --> 00:13:26.420
An important thing that's
going to follow us through

00:13:26.421 --> 00:13:32.960
the talk is the sensitivity
of a real valued query.

00:13:32.961 --> 00:13:34.250
Which is just going to be

00:13:34.251 --> 00:13:36.710
the maximum change
in that query f,

00:13:36.711 --> 00:13:39.510
if I were to change
one person's data.

00:13:39.511 --> 00:13:40.820
So I'm going to say what's

00:13:40.821 --> 00:13:43.815
the worst case over all
neighboring databases,

00:13:43.816 --> 00:13:49.310
how much can I change the
value of this function f.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:57.905
For these query classes
we're going to

00:13:57.906 --> 00:14:01.085
have Delta f be of size one over

00:14:01.086 --> 00:14:04.200
n for a database of size n.

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:11.850
Finally we want algorithms
that are going to be

00:14:11.851 --> 00:14:14.190
accurate and our accuracy notion

00:14:14.191 --> 00:14:16.940
is of this Alpha-Beta accuracy.

00:14:16.941 --> 00:14:19.355
So let's say that an algorithm M,

00:14:19.356 --> 00:14:21.770
is going to be
